    "Last week established a baseline: where you currently stand across the five dimensions of emotional intelligence. This week we move from diagnosis to practice. Understanding what emotional intelligence is has limited value compared to knowing how it operates in real situations — what triggers your emotional reactions, how those reactions move through your body and into your behavior, and most critically, what you can do in the moment to choose your response rather than surrender to it. Daniel Goleman's research identified self-regulation as the EQ dimension most consistently tied to leadership outcomes: leaders who can manage their emotional impulses produce teams that are more stable, more creative, and more willing to take the kinds of risks that produce organizational growth. Leaders who cannot regulate their emotions produce unpredictability, and unpredictability is one of the most expensive costs a team can bear. This week we study the mechanics of self-regulation in depth, examine the neuroscience of emotional triggers, and develop practical practices for interrupting the reactive patterns that are currently limiting your leadership effectiveness.",
