          "Walter Mischel's famous marshmallow experiments, conducted at Stanford in the late 1960s, followed children who were given a single marshmallow and told they could have a second one if they waited 15 minutes without eating the first. Follow-up studies tracked those children into adulthood and found that those who had successfully delayed gratification at age 4-5 consistently showed better life outcomes across a range of dimensions: higher SAT scores, lower BMI, lower rates of substance abuse, better social skills, and more robust responses to stress. Subsequent research by Mischel and others found that the capacity for delay was not primarily a matter of willpower — it was a matter of strategy. The children who successfully delayed had developed specific techniques: covering the marshmallow, turning away from it, thinking about something else entirely. The implication for leadership self-regulation is direct: the goal is not to want to react less, but to develop specific, practiced strategies that reliably interrupt the reactive pathway.",
